What "runtime proof" actually means

Your interpretation is basically right, but there's an important distinction.

Runtime does not simply mean "how long the program has been running."

In our context, runtime verification means actually executing the system and observing what it does.

For example:

Repository says:

Smart Note → Naya Note → PIS propagation → next Naya reads update.

That's designed/documented.

But runtime proof means we actually execute that path and observe:

Smart Note is requested.
Three note artifacts are created.
They are persisted.
Smart Links are returned.
Naya Note enters the PIS path.
PIS records the update.
The update appears in the appropriate intelligence feed/state.
A fresh Naya restores the system.
That Naya can actually retrieve the new knowledge.
The new knowledge influences the subsequent operation.

That is runtime proof.

GitHub Actions is one possible execution environment for that proof. It isn't synonymous with runtime itself.

